  "content": {
    "id": "p24-q1-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "1",
    "prompt": "Show that each of these functions is injective:",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ where $f(x)=3x+2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(a)=f(b)$ gives $3a+2=3b+2$, so $a=b$. Hence, $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Suppose $f(a)=f(b)$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}3a+2&=3b+2\\\\3a&=3b\\\\a&=b.\\end{align*}$$\nHence, $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 34
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$g=\\{(1,2),(2,3),(3,4),(4,1)\\}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The four distinct inputs have distinct outputs $2,3,4,1$. Hence, $g$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Since $g(1)=2$, $g(2)=3$, $g(3)=4$ and $g(4)=1$, distinct inputs produce distinct outputs. Hence, $g$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f:[0,\\infty)\\to\\mathbb{R}$ where $f(x)=x^2$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(a)=f(b)$ gives $a^2=b^2$. Since $a,b\\ge0$, $a=b$. Hence, $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Suppose $f(a)=f(b)$ for $a,b\\in[0,\\infty)$. Then\n$$a^2=b^2.$$\nSince $a,b\\ge0$, taking the non-negative square roots gives $a=b$. Hence, $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$g:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ where $g(x)=x^3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$g(a)=g(b)$ gives $a^3=b^3$, so $a=b$. Hence, $g$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Suppose $g(a)=g(b)$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}$. Then\n$$\\begin{align*}a^3&=b^3\\\\a&=b.\\end{align*}$$\nThe second equality follows by taking real cube roots. Hence, $g$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-q1-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f:\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{1\\}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ where $f(x)=\\dfrac{2}{x-1}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f(a)=f(b)$ gives $a-1=b-1$, so $a=b$. Hence, $f$ is injective.",
          "worked": "Suppose $f(a)=f(b)$ for $a,b\\in\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{1\\}$. Since both denominators are non-zero,\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{2}{a-1}&=\\frac{2}{b-1}\\\\2(b-1)&=2(a-1)\\\\b-1&=a-1\\\\b&=a.\\end{align*}$$\nHence, $f$ is injective.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      }
    ]
  },
